On 2008-12-07, Brian M. Dean <elzoog@yahoo.com> wrote:

> Nethack purists will deride me for using this, but I don't think doing 
> this is wrong.

It is not wrong as such, but as others have already told you, it's
cheating.

Cheating in nethack is like cheating in solitaire card games, only more
pointless. I sometimes cheat in solitaire games, just to avoid the
trouble of reshuffling the deck. But I have noticed it takes a large
part of the original appeal away from the games, and winning a game by
cheating is not nearly as satisfying as winning it by honest play, even
if the game is purely a game of chance.

Nethack is never purely a game of chance. There are random elements that
can lead to an unpreventable instant death in the game, but they're very
rare. With skillful play a player can ascend a very high percentage of
his or her games. And compared to a solitaire card game, there's no
additional fuss associated with starting a new game after death: The
computer does all the resuffling. In a way, a death in nethack is not a
real death. The character dies, yes, but the player is still alive and
has now more experience, and, hopefully, more skill than in the previous
game. And the early game in Nethack is fascinating enough to make it
worth it to try again.

YMMV, but I can't really see how save scumming could make nethack more
enjoyable. Yes, it would be a way to win the game quickly, without
having to learn the proper gameplay, but how is that any good? If you
want to savescum, it seems like you didn't really enjoy the game in the
first place; you see the game as something you want to finish quickly
and never look back. I'm not saying you shouldn't do it, but I can't see
why you would want to do that. If you do not enjoy nethack as it has
been designed (and not being able to restore a game after death is one
of the defining features of nethack), maybe you should be playing some
other game. There are even roguelikes that are balanced very differently
from the way nethack is balanced; maybe you should try Gearhead. In that
game you don't really die that often, and you tend to advance quickly.
(And Gearhead has all kind of neat mecha stuff, up to and including
nuclear warheads.) The downside is of course the fact that the game is
much more shallow and less thoughtfully balanced.

